# Arabic-Script Uyghur → Latin-Script Uyghur (ar_lat.rules)
#
# Source-Authors: Duval, J. R.; Janbaz, W. A.
# Source-Year: 2006
# Source-Title: An Introduction to Latin-Script Uyghur
# Source-Container: 2006 Middle East & Central Asia Politics, Economics, and Society Conference, University of Utah, Salt Lake City, 7-9 September 2006
# Source-Id: https://web.archive.org/web/20061201110649/http://www.uyghurdictionary.org/excerpts/An%20Introduction%20to%20LSU.pdf
#
# The target is Latin-Script Uyghur (LSU), also called Uyghur Latin Yeziqi
# (ULY), the standard made public on 3 July 2001 by the conferences held at
# Xinjiang University; Table 3 (p. 11) gives the ASU → LSU correspondence
# this file implements, and the second author sat on the committee that
# produced it.
#
# Corroborated: Saleh, I. & Janbaz, W. A. (2006) 'Web Development
# Considerations for Unicode-based Text Processing in Uyghur Language',
# 30th Internationalization and Unicode Conference, Washington DC, which
# gives the same account of the glottal-stop signs (p. 8) and writes LSU
# with é throughout its running text.
#
# Divergence on record: Mayer (2021), Issues in Uyghur backness harmony,
# Table 2.4 (p. 42), writes <ë> for /e/. He does not claim to use LSU, and
# notes that several Latin representations and informal variants circulate.
# Duval & Janbaz p. 9 records that ë was one of the three candidates the
# committee considered for ﺋﯥ and that é was the one chosen, so é is the
# standard and ë is a variant. This file follows the standard.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The two macros this file needs, for the three hamza-carrier rules below.
# The left context is Latin, not Arabic, because ICU matches a left context
# against text it has already transliterated; the right context is Arabic,
# because ICU matches that against source text it has not yet reached.
$LatCons = [b c d f g h j k l m n p q r s t w x y z] ;
$AsuVowel = [ا ە و ۇ ۆ ۈ ې ى] ;

# Contextual rules first. ICU takes the first rule that matches at a
# position rather than the longest, so every rule whose input is a letter
# in a context has to stand ahead of the plain rule for that same letter.

# The apostrophe as a reading guard (Duval & Janbaz pp. 12-13, rule 6).
# ﻥ+گ is not the single letter ڭ, and ﺱ+ھ is not the single letter ﺵ, and
# ڭ+ھ would read as one digraph plus a stray h. Each pair takes an
# apostrophe so the digraph reading is blocked: In'gliz, Is'haq, Chong'haji.
ن } گ > n'' ;
س } ھ > s'' ;
ڭ } ھ > ng'' ;

# The hamza carrier is not a letter of ASU: it is part of the initial form
# of every vowel, and is dropped there. Everywhere else it marks a hiatus,
# and LSU writes that hiatus as an apostrophe in two of the three places it
# can stand. After a consonant it is written (Qur'an, qet'i). Word-finally
# it is written, because there it carries the etymological ﻉ or ﺀ of an
# Arabic loan (wuqu', toghra'). Between two vowels it is not written, since
# two adjacent vowels already belong to two syllables (saet, qaide).
$LatCons { ئ > '' ;
ئ } [^$AsuVowel] > '' ;
ئ > ;

# consonants
ب > b ;  پ > p ;  ت > t ;  ج > j ;  چ > ch ;
ح > h ;  خ > x ;  د > d ;  ر > r ;  ز > z ;  س > s ;
ش > sh ; ص > s ;  ط > t ;  غ > gh ;
ف > f ;  ق > q ;  ك > k ;  گ > g ;  ل > l ;  م > m ;  ن > n ;
ه > h ;  ھ > h ;  ژ > zh ;  ڭ > ng ;  ۋ > w ;  ي > y ;

# ح, ص and ط are not letters of modern ASU. Duval & Janbaz note (p. 4 n. 14)
# that the letters written for purely Arabic phonemes were dropped from the
# 1920s on, so they appear only in unreformed spellings of Arabic-origin
# words; mapping them keeps such a word from passing through as Arabic.

# vowels
ا > a ;   ە > e ;   ۆ > ö ;   و > o ;
ۇ > u ;   ۈ > ü ;   ى > i ;   ې > é ;

# glottals
#
# ﺀ and ﻉ survive only in literary Arabic-origin spellings, where the
# hiatus they mark is written in LSU as an apostrophe (toghra', wuqu').
# The apostrophes are doubled because a bare one opens a quoted literal in
# the ICU rule syntax rather than emitting a character, which is how the
# rule for ﻉ was previously swallowed into the rule for ﺀ and lost.
ء > '' ;   ع > '' ;

:: NFC ;
